{
  "assessment": "Independent source-closure review confirms that the submitted Oracle is quantitatively faithful but the exact family is not yet closed at the Identification/Discovery boundary. Frozen-source lines 78-85 and 144-154 derive the self-similar law beta=1/(k+2), line 105 reports R(t) proportional to t^(1/5) for the k=3 experiment, and line 154 reports the k=1 simulation along t^(1/3). Because R(t)=A t^beta within one self-similar regime, the authored amplitude-free ratio R(t)/R(t_ref)=(t/t_ref)^beta is valid when both times share that regime; the unknown amplitude cancels rather than being set to one. The public q interval [1,10] is a conservative positive query interval, not a claimed acquisition range. Independent execution on 100,001 evenly spaced q values per branch reproduced q^(1/5) and q^(1/3) with zero floating-point discrepancy, remained finite and strictly increasing, reproduced every diagnostic/hidden prediction exactly, and returned bit-identical values on repeated calls. The Oracle programs share one power-law skeleton, their k=3 and k=1 conditions are source supported, historical/counterfactual public definitions are world-neutral after opaque-ID normalization, and known-truth submissions pass all four world-by-track cells with zero hidden-test scaled error. However, the actual CaseDefinition initial_observations tuple is shared by both tracks and states that the radius 'grows as a power of time'. This directly supplies the power-law family to Discovery, even though review_material.information_boundary labels that statement identification_only and the Discovery submission note promises that no candidate law family is supplied. The prior exact-exponent leakage was removed from the common fixed condition, but this remaining runtime-visible family disclosure still collapses blind family induction into exponent estimation.",
  "decision": "source_closure_rejected",
